[stderr] # Product brief — Mock Fantasy Quest 3
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Source of truth:** [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md). This document normalizes that
[stderr] brief for downstream runbooks; it does not resolve its open decisions.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Product problem and outcome
[stderr] 
[stderr] Casual adult players need a compact, approachable fantasy RPG that can be
[stderr] played through a complete story without a long commitment or dependable
[stderr] connection. The first useful session succeeds when a player completes the
[stderr] first turn-based battle and can explain how to save and resume. The release
[stderr] succeeds when a player can reach the short ending through the intended loop:
[stderr] explore, converse, accept/advance a quest, fight, manage rewards, save, and
[stderr] continue.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Category, audience, and experience
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Area | Normalized brief |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Category | Small single-player, 2D fantasy role-playing game (RPG). |
[stderr] | Users | Casual and adult players, including players using keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad, captions, adjustable text, or visual settings. |
[stderr] | First-release world | Exactly one town and one dungeon, connected by a guided short campaign. |
[stderr] | Core gameplay | Exploration, readable narrative dialogue, a party-based turn-based encounter, inventory/rewards, quest progression, and a short ending. |
[stderr] | Art | Original warm hand-painted fantasy with readable silhouettes and a restrained palette. |
[stderr] | Functional reference | *Final Fantasy V* is a scope reference for party-based turn-based adventure only, not a source of expressive material. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Selected target systems
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Target | First-release role | Input model | Delivery constraints | Out of scope |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Responsive public web application | Primary browser play surface for the complete game. | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad. | Responsive phone/tablet/desktop layouts; defined browser compatibility and web-performance budget are still to be approved. | Native desktop/mobile binaries; separate platform-exclusive gameplay. |
[stderr] | Progressive web app | Installable, offline-capable delivery of the same shared game core. | Same inputs, subject to browser/device support. | Offline assets and local save behavior; safe update/cache refresh without losing player data. | A distinct PWA-only campaign or a requirement for uninterrupted network access. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] The product uses a shared core and only necessary target adaptations. It must
[stderr] remain feasible on widely used devices and slow networks, but exact browser,
[stderr] device, storage, and performance floors require approval.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## First-release scope and measures
[stderr] 
[stderr] The release includes one complete town-to-dungeon-to-ending path, onboarding,
[stderr] at least one tutorialized turn-based battle, inventory, quests/progression,
[stderr] manual save/load and resume, checkpoints/recovery, settings/accessibility,
[stderr] audio/visual feedback, content/level delivery, crash/error recovery, and the
[stderr] explicitly requested account, multiplayer safety, live-service, and entitlement
[stderr] capabilities subject to the conflict decision below.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Measurable release measures:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - A new player can complete the tutorial battle, make a save, reload it, and
[stderr]   reach the ending on a supported target.
[stderr] - The shipped path contains one town, one dungeon, and one short ending; no
[stderr]   additional regions or campaign chapters are required for release.
[stderr] - Each selected target supports its stated input model, responsive/installable
[stderr]   behavior as applicable, and a tested offline/save/update recovery path.
[stderr] - Text, dialogue, inventory, quest, and progression interfaces remain readable
[stderr]   with the approved accessibility settings; captions and visual settings are
[stderr]   available wherever corresponding feedback is provided.
[stderr] - A restart after an interruption, recoverable error, or safe update presents a
[stderr]   player-facing recovery path and does not silently discard the last confirmed
[stderr]   save.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Later roadmap and non-goals
[stderr] 
[stderr] Deferred roadmap: live events/seasonal content, player-created/shared content
[stderr] (levels, mods, designs, stories), advanced analytics, and advanced
[stderr] monetisation. These are not release acceptance requirements.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Non-goals: copied branding, names, copy, artwork, audio, layouts, characters,
[stderr] world design, or distinctive interaction choreography from any reference;
[stderr] additional towns/dungeons/campaigns; native apps; and platform-specific game
[stderr] variants. No unsupported promise of cloud sync, payments, moderation coverage,
[stderr] or service uptime is implied until the relevant decisions are approved.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Constraints and assumptions
[stderr] 
[stderr] Explicit constraints are offline play, web/PWA delivery, accessibility,
[stderr] localisation, privacy, safe recovery, and original expression. Necessary safe
[stderr] assumptions: game content and UI will be original; the local device retains a
[stderr] durable save/cache when offline; remote features degrade visibly rather than
[stderr] blocking the core offline campaign; and observability/crash reports minimize
[stderr] personal data. These assumptions are design guardrails, not approval of a
[stderr] backend, vendor, account model, retention policy, or technical stack.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Decisions requiring human approval
[stderr] 
[stderr] 1. **Offline versus services:** reconcile the offline single-player premise
[stderr]    with required player identity, multiplayer/player safety, live-service, and
[stderr]    in-app purchase/entitlement handling. Decide whether those are release
[stderr]    requirements, optional connected enhancements, or deferred.
[stderr] 2. **Account and sync:** define whether accounts are mandatory, optional, or
[stderr]    absent for the core game; ownership/authentication; sync direction; conflict
[stderr]    handling; retention; deletion; and recovery when services are unavailable.
[stderr] 3. **Commerce and safety:** identify whether real-money purchases and
[stderr]    multiplayer are intended, their age/safety/moderation policy, applicable
[stderr]    legal/store obligations, and the supported regions.
[stderr] 4. **Technical and delivery baseline:** choose repository technology, supported
[stderr]    browsers/device classes, performance budget, offline storage quota, update
[stderr]    activation policy, and crash-reporting provider/data policy.
[stderr] 5. **Content balance:** approve party size, encounter count, quest count,
[stderr]    combat/progression rules, localisation languages, audio scope, and exact
[stderr]    accessibility options before production planning.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Reference boundary
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Prohibited from functional references | Permitted use |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Branding, names, logos, trademarks, marketing copy, story text, dialogue, characters, world/lore, assets, music, sound, layouts, screen composition, and interaction choreography | High-level functional scope: a compact party-based turn-based fantasy adventure. |
[stderr] | Reproducing distinctive combat screens, menus, flows, visual language, maps, quests, or progression presentation | Independently designed mechanics, content, UI, art, and feedback that meet this brief. |
