[stderr] # Generated projects
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each `supervisor initial` run creates one named workspace here and writes its
[stderr] project-specific `INITIAL.md`. The factory is run with the normal Supervisor
[stderr] collection command; there is no special `generate-runbooks` command.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor-run --project <project-name>
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each project workspace contains:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - `PROJECT_BRIEF.md`: normalized source brief;
[stderr] - `INITIAL.md`: the user-created source brief supplied to `supervisor-run`;
[stderr] - `specification/`: canonical requirements, platform, experience, and domain
[stderr]   specifications;
[stderr] - `planning/`: the complete work checklist, dependency graph, and plan for
[stderr]   creating the R files in manageable batches;
[stderr] - `authoring-runbooks/`: generated B-series runbook-writing files. Each writes
[stderr]   no more than seven R-series files; B files write Markdown instructions, not
[stderr]   product code;
[stderr] - `runbooks/`: generated R-series files: detailed instructions for the actual
[stderr]   implementation and verification work;
[stderr] - `.supervisor/`: isolated run state for the generated collections.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The relationship is deliberately layered:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md
[stderr]   → repository F001–F016 factory files (with --project <project-name>)
[stderr]   → projects/<project-name>/authoring-runbooks/B....md
[stderr]   → projects/<project-name>/runbooks/R....md
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] The F-series files are reusable source factory instructions. They create the
[stderr] project folder, specification, complete work checklist, and first B-series
[stderr] files. A B-series file is a *runbook writer*: it takes one small part of that
[stderr] checklist and writes no more than seven R-series Markdown files. “Bounded” is
[stderr] only that size limit. It does not build the application. An R-series file is a
[stderr] *product-work instruction*: it contains the detailed steps and success evidence
[stderr] for one small piece of real product work.
[stderr] 
[stderr] In other words, the step that creates the R files is not an F-series file:
[stderr] `F014` creates the first B files, then running each B file creates its R files.
[stderr] 
[stderr] For a very large product, a B dispatcher file creates more B-series runbook
[stderr] writers for the remaining areas of the checklist. This is how the collection
[stderr] scales to hundreds or thousands of R-series files without oversized prompts or
[stderr] a separate manual run. The parent collection explicitly registers its generated
[stderr] child collections, so the originating `--run-all` invocation follows them
[stderr] automatically.
[stderr] 
[stderr] `supervisor-run --project <project-name>` is the normal way to start or resume
[stderr] one project. It reads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs the factory source
[stderr] collection, follows only child collections belonging to that workspace, and
[stderr] stores factory task state in `projects/<project-name>/.supervisor/factory.sqlite3`.
[stderr] Accepted tasks are skipped on later invocations; the first unfinished task is
[stderr] resumed using its existing collection state.
